1‑wide stripe, a 4‑wide block a 2‑wide stripe, a 3×3 block just its centre, and the corridors (1 cell wide) never change. Every rectangle belonging to the same connected structure receives the same colour – the colour of the seed in that structure. The seed cell itself stays where it is (it is already inside the interior).\n\nSo the rule: *for each connected non‑background component, find its single non‑blue seed cell; paint every cell of the component whose 8 neighbours are all non‑background in the seed colour.* A cell with all 8 neighbours filled is exactly an interior cell of a rectangle (a corridor cell or a block border always has an empty neighbour).\n\nI checked this formulation programmatically against all three train pairs: zero mismatching cells in each.\n\n## Applying it to the test input\n\nThe test has three structures: the top one (seed 4 at (2,10)) with blocks around rows 1–11, the middle one (seed 8 at (14,16)) including the big 7×12 block at rows 19–25, and the bottom one (seed 6 at (21,27)) spanning the long row 28 corridor. The program fills every block interior of each structure in 4, 8 and 6 respectively; the big block's interior becomes a 5×10 area of 8, and the bottom structure's 4×4 blocks each get a 2×2 patch of 6, the 3‑wide ones a 1‑wide stripe. The shape colour, seed colours and background are all read from the grid, so nothing is specific to the train pairs.\n\n## Program\n\nThe construction is staged one structure at a time: each emit shows one connected structure with all its block interiors painted.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    mask = g != bg\n    # the structure colour is the most common non-background colour\n    vals = [c for c in np.unique(g) if c != bg]\n    shape_c = max(vals, key=lambda c: (g == c).sum())\n    # interior cells: all 8 neighbours are non-background\n    pad = np.zeros((H + 2, W + 2), bool)\n    pad[1:-1, 1:-1] = mask\n    inner = np.ones((H, W), bool)\n    for dy in (-1, 0, 1):\n        for dx in (-1, 0, 1):\n            inner &= pad[1 + dy:H + 1 + dy, 1 + dx:W + 1 + dx]\n    for comp in components(mask):\n        seeds = [g[y, x] for y, x in comp if g[y, x] != shape_c]\n        if not seeds:\n            continue\n        colour = seeds[0]\n        for y, x in comp:\n            if inner[y, x]:\n                out[y, x] = colour\n        emit(out, \"Paint the interior (block minus its 1-cell border) of every \"\n                  \"rectangle in this connected structure with its seed colour %d\" % colour)\n    emit(out, \"All structures filled: final result\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":" and output (the fact sheet's changed cells confirm this), only blue cells become coloured, and the recoloured cells are always the **interior** of a rectangle: the block minus its 1‑cell border."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"ged cells confirm this), only blue cells become coloured, and the recoloured cells are always the **interior** of a rectangle: the block minus its 1‑cell border."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The seed cell itself stays where it is (it is already inside the interior)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"bours are all non‑background in the seed colour.* A cell with all 8 neighbours filled is exactly an interior cell of a rectangle (a corridor cell or a block border always has an empty neighbour)."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each structure is a set of solid rectangles (blocks) joined to each other by 1‑cell‑wide corridors, and somewhere inside exactly one of its rectangles sits a single odd‑coloured cell (3, 4, 2, 6 …)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"de block gets a 1‑wide stripe, a 4‑wide block a 2‑wide stripe, a 3×3 block just its centre, and the corridors (1 cell wide) never change."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"the seed colour.* A cell 